['```java
import java.util.ResourceBundle;
import java.io.FileInputStream;
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class CustomResourceBundleLoader {
    public static int load(String path) throws Exception {
        ResourceBundle bundle = ResourceBundle.getBundle("CustomBundle", new FileInputLoadControl(path));
        Map<String, String> properties = new LinkedHashMap<>();
        for (String key : bundle.getKeys()) {
            properties.put(key, bundle.getString(key));
        }
        return properties.size();
    }
}

class FileInputLoadControl extends Control implements InputStreamBundle.Control {
    private String filePath;

    public FileInputLoadControl(String filePath) {
        this.filePath = filePath;
    }

    @Override
    public InputStream getInputStream() throws IOException {
        return new FileInputStream(filePath);
    }
}
```

The above code defines a custom `CustomResourceBundleLoader` class that loads a resource bundle from a file and returns the number of items in the bundle. The class uses Java's standard library functions, including `ResourceBundle`, `FileInputStream`, and maps to accomplish this task. The `load()` method takes a path to the resource file as input and returns the number of items as output.

The `CustomResourceBundleLoader` uses an inner class named `FileInputLoadControl` that extends Java's built-in `Control` interface, implementing the `InputStreamBundle.Control` interface for reading resources from files. The `getInputStream()` method is defined to return a new `FileInputStream` object using the specified file path.']

