[" Here is a basic structure for the Node.js module, using `fs-extra` for file operations, `axios` for logging to an external service, and `async` for handling concurrent operations efficiently. This example assumes you are using Winston (a popular logging library) for logging, but you can replace it with any other logging service of your choice.

```javascript
const fs = require('fs-extra');
const axios = require('axios');
const async = require('async');
const path = require('path');
const { createLogger, format, transports } = require('winston');

// Configure your logging service here
const logger = createLogger({
  level: 'info',
  format: format.combine(
    format.timestamp(),
    format.json()
  ),
  defaultMeta: { service: 'FileReadingModule' },
  transports: [
    new transports.Console()
  ]
});

class FileReader {
  constructor(options) {
    this.directory = options.directory;
    this.loggingService = options.loggingService || logger;
    this.maxRetries = options.maxRetries || 3;
    this.retryInterval = options.retryInterval || 5000;
    this.concurrencyLimit = options.concurrencyLimit || 10;
  }

  async readFiles() {
    try {
      const files = await fs.readdir(this.directory);
      return await this._processFiles(files);
    } catch (error) {
      this.loggingService.error(`Failed to read directory: ${error}`);
      throw error;
    }
  }

  async _processFiles(files, callback) {
    const tasks = files.map((file) => () =>
      this._readFile(file)
        .catch((err) => {
          if (this.maxRetries > 0) {
            setTimeout(() => {
              this._processFiles([file], callback);
            }, this.retryInterval);
            this.loggingService.error(`Failed to read file ${file}: ${err}`);
          } else {
            this.loggingService.error(`Gave up on file ${file}: ${err}`);
          }
        })
    );

    await async.limitConcurrentLimit(tasks, this.concurrencyLimit, (err) => {
      if (err) {
        this.loggingService.error(`Failed processing files: ${err}`);
      } else {
        callback();
      }
    });
  }

  async _readFile(file) {
    try {
      const stats = await fs.stat(path.join(this.directory, file));
      if (stats.isDirectory()) {
        throw new Error(`Specified path is a directory: ${file}`);
      }
      if (stats.size > 100 * 1024 * 1024) {
        throw new Error(`File size exceeds limit: ${file}`);
      }

      const content = await fs.readFile(path.join(this.directory, file), 'utf8');
      this.loggingService.info(`Successfully read file ${file}: ${content.length} bytes`);

      // Perform any additional operations here and log them if necessary

      // Log to external service
      await axios.post('YOUR_LOGGING_API_URL', {
        file: file,
        content: content,
        size: stats.size
      });
    } catch (error) {
      this.loggingService.error(`Failed to read file ${file}: ${error}`);
      throw error;
    }
  }
}

module.exports = FileReader;
```

Documentation for future developers:

```markdown
# FileReadingModule Node.js Module

## Overview

This module asynchronously reads files from a specified directory and logs metadata about each read operation to an external logging service. The module handles files up to 100 MB in size, managing memory efficiently to avoid excessive resource consumption. It is designed for optimal performance and user experience when dealing with directories containing thousands of files.

## Setup

Install the required dependencies:
```sh
npm install fs-extra axios async winston
```

Customize the module by configuring your logging service in the constructor. The current implementation uses Winston as the logging library, but it can be easily replaced with other logging services of your choice.

## Usage

```javascript
const FileReader = require('./FileReadingModule');

(async () => {
  try {
    const reader = new FileReader({
      directory: '/path/to/your/directory',
      loggingService: YOUR_LOGGING_SERVICE // Configure your logging service here
    });

    await reader.readFiles();
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(`Error occurred while reading files: ${error}`);
  }
})();
```

## Configuration

Configure the module by providing options in the constructor:
- `directory`: The directory to read files from
- `loggingService`: The logging service to use (default is Winston)
- `maxRetries`: The maximum number of retries for failed operations before giving up (default is 3)
- `retryInterval`: The interval between retries in milliseconds (default is 5000)
- `concurrencyLimit`: The maximum number of concurrent file reads (default is 10)

## Performance Tracking and Integration with Cloud Monitoring Systems

Integrate the module with a cloud-based monitoring system for real-time performance tracking by modifying the logging service configuration to send metrics to your preferred monitoring service.

## Maintainability and Adaptability

The module is designed with a modular, maintainable structure that can be easily extended to integrate additional systems, such as alerting or notification services.
```"]

[' Due to the complexity of your request, I'll provide a high-level outline of the code structure for `updateUserPassword` function in PHP. The given solution assumes you have already set up your microservices architecture, third-party services (MFA, distributed task queue), and logging/monitoring systems.

```php
function updateUserPassword(int $userId, string $password): int {
    try {
        // MFA verification
        $mfa = new \App\Authentication\MFA($userId);
        $result = $mfa->verifyToken();

        if (!$result) return -2;

        // Cache user data for performance
        $cacheKey = 'user_' . $userId;
        $userData = cache($cacheKey, function() use ($userId) {
            return \App\Database\ConnectionPool::get()->query(
                "SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = :id", [':id' => $userId]
            )->fetch();
        });

        if (!$userData) return 0; // User not found

        // Check password policy compliance
        if (!preg_match('/(?=.*[A-Z])(?=.*[a-z])(?=.*\d)(?=.*[@$!%*?&])(?!.*\s).{12,}/', $password)) {
            return 0; // Password does not meet policy requirements
        }

        // Database transaction for updating user password and session invalidation
        \App\Database\ConnectionPool::get()->transaction(function($connection) use ($userId, $password) {
            $stmt = $connection->prepare("UPDATE users SET password = :password WHERE id = :id");
            $stmt->execute([':password' => password_hash($password, PASSWORD_DEFAULT), ':id' => $userId]);
            $sessionKey = $userData['session_key'];
            $connection->query("DELETE FROM sessions WHERE key = :key", [':key' => $sessionKey]);
        });

        // Asynchronously update password using a distributed task queue
        \App\Queue\TaskProducer::publish('passwordUpdate', [$userId, $password]);

        return 1; // Password updated successfully
