3. Example code snippets:
   - Format negotiation: In the `CloudStorageController`, use a header or query parameter to determine the desired data format, and defer to the most suitable format based on performance and compatibility considerations.

```java
@GetMapping("/{objectId}")
public ResponseEntity<Object> getObject(@PathVariable("objectId") UUID objectId, @RequestHeader(value = "Accept", required = false) String accept) {
    // Determine the most suitable format for this client based on performance, compatibility, and data size considerations
    StorageObject storageObject = cloudStorageService.getObject(objectId);
    Object objectToReturn;

    if (accept != null && isSupportedFormat(accept)) {
        // Use the preferred format if available
        objectToReturn = deserialize(storageObject, accept);
    } else {
        // If no suitable format was specified, return the default format
        objectToReturn = storageObject;
    }

    // Set appropriate response headers for caching and content type
    HttpHeaders headers = new HttpHeaders();
    headers.setCacheControl(CacheControl.maxAge(3600).getHeaderValue());
    headers.setContentType(MediaType.valueOf(storageObject.getClass().getName()));

    return ResponseEntity.ok().headers(headers).body(objectToReturn);
}
```

- Caching policies: In the `CachingService`, implement heuristic-based expiration strategies or versioning schemes to mitigate the risk of serving stale data while maintaining acceptable performance levels.

