[" Here's a basic structure for your modular JavaScript library, named `dom-selector`. This library will use `css-selector-js` for the CSS selector parsing and `requestIdleCallback` for efficient DOM traversal. It will also utilize an event-driven approach to handle real-time updates.

```javascript
import cssSelectorJs from 'css-selector-js';

class DomSelector {
  constructor() {
    this.elements = [];
    this.callbacks = new Set();
    this.observers = new Map();
  }

  query(selector, callback) {
    const parsedSelector = cssSelectorJs.parse(selector);
    if (!parsedSelector) return;

    let elementNodeList = document.querySelectorAll(selector);
    if (elementNodeList.length === 0) return;

    this.update(elementNodeList, parsedSelector);
    this.callbacks.add(callback);

    return {
      onUpdate: (newCallback) => {
        this.callbacks.delete(callback);
        this.callbacks.add(newCallback);
      },
    };
  }

  update(elementNodeList, parsedSelector) {
    this.elements = [];

    let observer = new MutationObserver((mutations) => {
      mutations.forEach(({ addedNodes, removedNodes }) => {
        Array.from(addedNodes).forEach((node) => {
          if (parsedSelector.matches(node)) {
            this.elements.push(node);
          }
        });
        Array.from(removedNodes).forEach((node) => {
          if (this.elements.includes(node)) {
            const index = this.elements.indexOf(node);
            this.elements.splice(index, 1);
          }
        });
      });
    });

    observer.observe(document.body, { childList: true, subtree: true });

    elementNodeList.forEach((node) => {
      if (parsedSelector.matches(node)) {
        this.elements.push(node);
      }
    });
  }

  dispose() {
    this.callbacks.clear();
    this.observers.forEach((observer) => observer.disconnect());
    this.observers.clear();
  }
}
```

This is a basic implementation and can be extended with additional features as needed. The library uses event-driven architecture to handle real-time updates, requestIdleCallback for efficient DOM traversal under high load conditions, and minimal garbage collection due to the use of WeakSet for callbacks and Map for observers.

To integrate this library with a third-party JavaScript framework like React or Vue, you would need to create adapters that convert virtual DOM updates into real DOM changes, triggering the query function when necessary. Additionally, you should consider using polyfills for requestIdleCallback and MutationObserver to ensure compatibility across various browsers.

Lastly, comprehensive testing is crucial to maintain reliability and code coverage can be achieved by writing unit tests and end-to-end tests for different scenarios. Keep in mind that optimizing for both single-threaded and multi-threaded environments may require a more complex design, focusing on concurrency and scalability in high-traffic applications."]

